    ",{\"vid\":\"JtKAScORevQ\",\"title\":\"How our voting system (and IRV) betrays your favourite candidate\",\"channel\":\"The Center for Election Science\",\"summary\":\"The Center for Election Science explains favorite betrayal in instant-runoff voting: in some elections, ranking your favorite first can cause your least favorite to win. It contrasts this with plurality voting's spoiler problem.\",\"why_hn\":\"The standard link in ranked-choice voting threads to show IRV does not eliminate the spoiler effect.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"voting systems\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"instant-runoff voting\",\"ranked choice\",\"favorite betrayal\",\"spoiler effect\",\"approval voting\"],\"quote\":\"here's a video explaining the strategic voting issue\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"KBcxuM-qXec\",\"title\":\"The Moon's Orbit is WEIRD\",\"channel\":\"minutephysics\",\"summary\":\"A minutephysics explainer showing that the Moon's path around the Sun is not a spiral but closer to a 12-sided polygon with curved corners. It argues the Moon arguably orbits the Sun rather than the Earth.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it whenever the Moon's orbit comes up, as a concise explanation of why the Moon always curves toward the Sun.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"moon orbit\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"orbital mechanics\",\"moon\",\"earth\",\"solar system\",\"gravity\"],\"quote\":\"I can't explain better than minute physics, highly recommend\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"KFNxJVTJleE\",\"title\":\"How Gamers Killed Ultima Online's Virtual Ecology | War Stories | Ars Technica\",\"channel\":\"Ars Technica\",\"summary\":\"An Ars Technica War Stories episode in which Richard Garriott and Starr Long describe Ultima Online's planned virtual ecology and how players wrecked it. Players slaughtered the animals and also broke the in-game economy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a canonical anecdote that designers badly predicted player behavior, and in threads about UO and sharding.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mmo game design\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"ultima online\",\"richard garriott\",\"virtual ecology\",\"player behavior\",\"game history\"],\"quote\":\"Instead the players indiscriminately killed both the carnivores and the herbivores, because the players were human.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"KSWqx8goqSY\",\"title\":\"DEFCON 17: That Awesome Time I Was Sued For Two Billion Dollars\",\"channel\":\"Christiaan008\",\"summary\":\"Jason Scott of textfiles.com tells the story at DEFCON 17 of being sued for two billion dollars by a scammer who plagued him and others for over a decade. The talk is tragic and funny, covering what he learned about misleading schemes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a great, hilarious talk about legal threats and cease and desist letters.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"legal threats and scams\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"jason scott\",\"defcon\",\"lawsuit\",\"textfiles\",\"internet history\"],\"quote\":\"Watch his talk on the matter, it is really great\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"K_q-xnYRugQ\",\"title\":\"Perhaps the weakest link in the US electrical system\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains weaknesses in the US electrical system, centering on how cord and circuit ratings can mismatch and cause fires. It covers extension cords, power strips and plug history in about 25 minutes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a clear explanation of why under-rated extension cords and daisy-chained power strips are dangerous.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us electrical system safety\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\"],\"tags\":[\"extension cords\",\"power strips\",\"electrical safety\",\"plugs\",\"amp ratings\"],\"quote\":\"here's a 25 minute mini-documentary of the US electrical system's history and problems\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"KaOC9danxNo\",\"title\":\"Space Oddity\",\"channel\":\"Rare Earth\",\"summary\":\"Commander Chris Hadfield performs a revised version of David Bowie's Space Oddity, recorded aboard the International Space Station. It was released as a music video in 2013.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it for the sense of wonder and chills it gives, and bring it up in threads about Bowie, space and astronauts.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"space oddity cover from the iss\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"chris hadfield\",\"david bowie\",\"international space station\",\"music video\",\"astronaut\"],\"quote\":\"It gives me chills every time I listen to it.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"KqFNbCcyFkk\",\"title\":\"The Fall of The Simpsons: How it Happened\",\"channel\":\"Super Eyepatch Wolf\",\"summary\":\"Super Eyepatch Wolf analyzes why The Simpsons declined after its golden age, pointing to changes in the writers' room and a shift in the style and complexity of its comedy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive explanation of the Simpsons decline, recommended in every thread asking when the golden age ended.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"decline of the simpsons\",\"categories\":[\"Film analysis & criticism\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"the simpsons\",\"tv writing\",\"comedy\",\"zombie simpsons\",\"media analysis\"],\"quote\":\"For an actual exploration of what happened, I highly recommend this video.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"L-x8DYTOv7w\",\"title\":\"YouTubers have to declare ads. Why doesn't anyone else?\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott examines why YouTubers must declare paid endorsements while TV, film and other media face no such rule, comparing regulations in the UK and US.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a clear explainer on sponsorship and ad disclosure rules and their inconsistency across media.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"advertising disclosure rules\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Product, marketing & sales\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"product placement\",\"influencer regulation\",\"sponsorship\",\"tom scott\",\"ftc\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott has a great video on the inconsistency that online influencers and youtubers have to declare ads/product-placements while traditional media does not.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"LWGJA9i18Co\",\"title\":\"OK Go - Upside Down & Inside Out\",\"channel\":\"OKGoVEVO\",\"summary\":\"OK Go's music video for Upside Down & Inside Out, filmed during parabolic flights so the band and performers are weightless in the plane.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as a creative music video shot entirely in zero gravity, citing it in threads about parabolic flights and space.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"zero-gravity music video\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"ok go\",\"parabolic flight\",\"zero gravity\",\"music video\"],\"quote\":\"One of my preferred video clip ever was filmed entirely during a parabolic flight\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"M4AhABManTw\",\"title\":\"RAT POISON AND BRANDY: THE 1904 ST. LOUIS OLYMPIC MARATHON.\",\"channel\":\"Jon Bois\",\"summary\":\"Jon Bois tells the story of the 1904 St. Louis Olympic marathon, a chaotic race involving fraud, rat poison, brandy, feral dogs and near-deaths. It is an episode of his show Pretty Good.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a funny, well-told retelling of one of the strangest sporting events ever.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sports history\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Sports & athletics\"],\"tags\":[\"jon bois\",\"olympics\",\"marathon\",\"storytelling\",\"1904\"],\"quote\":\"He has a video on the marathon that's very worth the 21 minutes\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"MFzDaBzBlL0\",\"title\":\"The Backwards Brain Bicycle - Smarter Every Day 133\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"Destin of Smarter Every Day learns to ride a bicycle whose steering is reversed, taking about eight months, and compares it with how his young son learned much faster. It illustrates knowledge versus understanding and brain plasticity in adults versus children.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it widely in discussions of tacit knowledge, habit, unlearning and adult versus child learning.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"learning and neuroplasticity\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"backwards bicycle\",\"tacit knowledge\",\"neuroplasticity\",\"motor learning\",\"smarter every day\"],\"quote\":\"There is an absolutely fascinating video about plasticity of learning between adults and children that I'd highly recommend watching [1].\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"MQyxG4vTyZ8\",\"title\":\"China is erasing its border with Hong Kong\",\"channel\":\"Vox\",\"summary\":\"A Vox Borders episode with Johnny Harris explaining how China is gradually erasing the line between mainland China and Hong Kong since the 1997 handover. It describes the one country, two systems arrangement and its expiration date.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a concise, captivating introduction to the Hong Kong conflict during the 2019 extradition-law protests.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"hong kong and china relations\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"hong kong\",\"china\",\"one country two systems\",\"vox borders\",\"johnny harris\"],\"quote\":\"At 15 minutes, it's a captivating introduction to the conflict.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"MjdpR-TY6QU\",\"title\":\"How to Cure Aging – During Your Lifetime?\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"A Kurzgesagt animated explainer on whether aging could be cured within our lifetimes, made with Lifespan.io and the LEAF foundation. It introduces the idea of treating aging as a medical problem.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible introduction to longevity research that changed some commenters' views.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"longevity research\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"aging\",\"longevity\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"biotech\",\"animated explainer\"],\"quote\":\"Yes this is a great introduction into the topic.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Mw9qiV7XlFs\",\"title\":\"★ Cab ride 🇨🇭St. Moritz - 🇮🇹Tirano (Bernina pass), Switzerland to Italy [10.2019]\",\"channel\":\"lorirocks777\",\"summary\":\"A two-hour driver's-eye cab ride along the Bernina railway from St. Moritz in Switzerland to Tirano in Italy. The UNESCO-listed line crosses the Alps at high altitude with steep adhesion gradients.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as relaxing slow-TV, a long scenic video to unwind with.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"train cab rides\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Arts & entertainment (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"slow tv\",\"bernina railway\",\"switzerland\",\"alps\",\"relaxing\"],\"quote\":\"Some of my favorite long ones. They are incredibly relaxing.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Mz3JeYfBTcY\",\"title\":\"Emily Shea - \\\"Perl Out Loud\\\"\",\"channel\":\"The Perl and Raku Conference - Greenville, SC 2026\",\"summary\":\"Emily Shea, a Fastly engineer with repetitive strain injury, describes using voice dictation as her main way of programming. She covers homophones, custom vocabulary and building her own voice commands to write Perl.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a phenomenal demonstration that voice-driven development is viable and as an eye-opener on accessibility-unfriendly coding habits.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"voice programming\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"voice coding\",\"rsi\",\"accessibility\",\"perl\",\"dictation\"],\"quote\":\"This video from the article is a phenomenal demonstration of dictation coding\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"N-yALPEpV4w\",\"title\":\"Why renewables can’t save the planet | Michael Shellenberger | TEDxDanubia\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Michael Shellenberger argues in a TEDx talk that solar and wind need huge amounts of land and mining, and says nuclear power is a better path to protecting both the climate and nature.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters credit it with changing their minds on nuclear power and cite it for the downsides of renewables.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear versus renewable energy\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"nuclear power\",\"renewables\",\"land use\",\"climate change\",\"tedx\"],\"quote\":\"it's what changed my mind and therefore in my opinion the best delivery of the argument I'm aware of.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NKlbE2eROC0\",\"title\":\"Did Nintendo really forget to Optimize Super Mario 64 ? | MVG\",\"channel\":\"Modern Vintage Gamer\",\"summary\":\"Modern Vintage Gamer examines the claim from the Super Mario 64 decompilation project that the game shipped unoptimized, and puts it in the context of 1996 N64 development.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a well-researched rebuttal of the idea that Nintendo simply forgot an optimization flag.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"super mario 64 optimization\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"super mario 64\",\"n64\",\"decompilation\",\"compiler optimization\",\"retro gaming\"],\"quote\":\"ModernVintageGamer did a video discussing the topic that's pretty well-researched\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NMvwHDFVpCE\",\"title\":\"Andres Duany Lecture. Suburban Sprawl.\",\"channel\":\"Andriy Kosetsky\",\"summary\":\"A recording of Andres Duany's 1991 'San Antonio By Design' seminar on suburban sprawl. He lays out how codes, zoning and policy produce sprawl and makes the case for new urbanism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a comprehensive, still-accurate and often funny account of how zoning and policy force suburban sprawl.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"suburban sprawl\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"new urbanism\",\"zoning\",\"urban planning\",\"andres duany\",\"housing policy\"],\"quote\":\"His talk from 30 years ago is still so eerily precise and comprehensive\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NUQu-OPrzUc\",\"title\":\"2012 Burnett Lecture Part 1 Keynote Speaker: Russell A. Barkley, Ph.D.\",\"channel\":\"UNC Learning Center\",\"summary\":\"Russell Barkley gives the keynote at UNC's 2012 Burnett Seminar on ADHD. He presents it as an executive function disorder and discusses its effects and how to help.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a rigorous clinical look at ADHD, including the view that it has no benefits and that external prompts help.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"adhd and executive function\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"adhd\",\"russell barkley\",\"executive function\",\"psychology\",\"accountability\"],\"quote\":\"Educate yourself by watching this\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NXN7TOg3kj4\",\"title\":\"FreeCAD 0.20 For Beginners | 1 | Setup , preferences and navigation | Easy Tutorial for Learning CAD\",\"channel\":\"MangoJelly Solutions for FreeCAD\",\"summary\":\"The first lesson of MangoJelly's FreeCAD 0.20 beginner course, covering setup, preferences and navigation. The series teaches fundamentals through complete workflows such as sketching, Part Design and STL export.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an excellent beginner series that fills FreeCAD's gap in learning material.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"freecad\",\"categories\":[\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"freecad\",\"cad\",\"3d modeling\",\"beginner tutorial\",\"3d printing\"],\"quote\":\"MangoJelly does an excellent work on that.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NYSgd1XSZXc\",\"title\":\"Taipei 101 Damper movement on 12 May 08\",\"channel\":\"phuaalvin\",\"summary\":\"Tourist footage of the 660-ton tuned mass damper in Taipei 101 swaying during tremors from the 2008 Sichuan earthquake. The damper hangs from the 92nd floor.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever skyscraper dampers come up, since it shows how huge the moving mass is.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tuned mass dampers\",\"categories\":[\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Geology & earth science\"],\"tags\":[\"taipei 101\",\"earthquake\",\"skyscrapers\",\"structural engineering\"],\"quote\":\"There's a good video of it moving that gives you an idea of just how colossal these things are\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"NaL_Cb42WyY\",\"title\":\"Pi hiding in prime regularities\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown shows how pi emerges from prime numbers via complex numbers and sums of squares, culminating in a formula for approximating pi.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as a beautiful, understandable proof and a favorite among 3Blue1Brown videos.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"number theory\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"pi\",\"prime numbers\",\"complex numbers\",\"gaussian integers\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"This is a really beautiful proof that connects complex numbers and primes to derive a formula for calculating pi.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"O-2tpwW0kmU\",\"title\":\"Sci-Fi Short Film “Slaughterbots” | DUST\",\"channel\":\"DUST\",\"summary\":\"Slaughterbots is a short sci-fi film in which swarms of cheap autonomous drones are programmed to kill targets by profile. It was made as arms-control advocacy against autonomous weapons.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps citing it whenever drone swarms, autonomous weapons or AI warfare come up, as a believable depiction of where this leads.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"autonomous weapons\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Robotics\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"slaughterbots\",\"drone swarms\",\"ai weapons\",\"arms control\",\"sci-fi short\"],\"quote\":\"which depicts exactly this in harrowing detail\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"OMPfEXIlTVE\",\"title\":\"RailsConf 2015 - Nothing is Something\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Sandi Metz argues that hidden assumptions in code are missing concepts, and shows how to name and expose them so code is easier to understand and change. It covers null objects and messages in Ruby.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a funny, smart and well-constructed talk on abstraction, duplication and object-oriented design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object-oriented design\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"sandi metz\",\"ruby\",\"abstraction\",\"null object\",\"refactoring\"],\"quote\":\"as a talk it is fantastic - funny, smart, well put together.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"OQ5jsbhAv_M\",\"title\":\"Lecture 19: Dynamic Programming I: Fibonacci, Shortest Paths\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"Erik Demaine's MIT 6.006 lecture introducing dynamic programming through Fibonacci numbers and shortest paths. It covers memoization, subproblems and optimal substructure.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear introduction to dynamic programming from a well-regarded instructor.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"dynamic programming\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"algorithms\",\"memoization\",\"shortest paths\",\"mit ocw\",\"erik demaine\"],\"quote\":\"Please watch this MIT intro\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Oo0jm1PPRuo\",\"title\":\"How We're Redefining the kg\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains why the kilogram is being redefined in terms of Planck's constant rather than a physical metal artifact. It visits NIST to show the Kibble balance and why measuring mass precisely is hard.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as an excellent, accessible account of why the kilogram's definition changed, in redefinition threads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"kilogram redefinition\",\"categories\":[\"Physics & space (other)\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"kilogram\",\"planck constant\",\"kibble balance\",\"metrology\",\"nist\"],\"quote\":\"I found the Veritasium video to be a bit more interesting for the \\\"why\\\" aspect of this change\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"OqjcrfKjobY\",\"title\":\"Tabs Outliner Overview\",\"channel\":\"omnray\",\"summary\":\"A short overview of Tabs Outliner, a Chrome extension that shows browser tabs and windows as a nestable tree. It demonstrates organizing, saving and restoring tabs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters who struggle with tab overload use it to show what tree-based tab management looks like and recommend the extension.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"browser tab management\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"tabs outliner\",\"chrome extension\",\"tab management\",\"productivity\",\"browser\"],\"quote\":\"Tabs Outliner puts every tab as a leaf in a tree, and you can nest leaves\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PIPjmmmh_os\",\"title\":\"Slavoj Žižek + Paul Holdengräber \\\"Surveillance and whistleblowers\\\" - International Authors' Stage\",\"channel\":\"Det Kgl. Bibliotek\",\"summary\":\"Slavoj Zizek and Paul Holdengraber discuss surveillance and whistleblowers at the International Authors' Stage, ranging over Snowden, Assange, Pussy Riot and humor.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it an entertaining, complementary philosophical take on surveillance, with a funny story about jamming secret police.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"surveillance and whistleblowers\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"zizek\",\"surveillance\",\"snowden\",\"assange\",\"philosophy\"],\"quote\":\"I think Slavoj Žižek and Paul Holdengräber on \\\"Surveillance and whistleblowers\\\" is worth a watch.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PJnJ8mK3Q3g\",\"title\":\"Plastic Recycling is an Actual Scam | Climate Town\",\"channel\":\"Climate Town\",\"summary\":\"Climate Town gives a comedic, fast-paced account of how the plastics industry promoted recycling, including the resin-code recycling symbol, while knowing most plastic could not be economically recycled. It points viewers to the Frontline documentary Plastic Wars for a deeper treatment.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters send it as a short, entertaining explanation of why people believe plastic is recyclable and how industry PR created that belief.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"plastic recycling myth\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"plastic recycling\",\"recycling symbol\",\"industry lobbying\",\"greenwashing\",\"climate town\"],\"quote\":\"This video is under 10mins and features a lot of the PR adverts.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PQccNdwm8Tw\",\"title\":\"How technology loses out in companies, countries & continents and what to do about it\",\"channel\":\"bert hubert\",\"summary\":\"A conference presentation by Bert Hubert on how companies lose their technological edge and become outsourcing hubs. It argues the same dynamic applies to whole countries and continents, and ends with suggestions for reversing it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a sharper explanation of why outsourcing and specialization erode deep expertise, in threads on Intel, Boeing and software decline.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"loss of technological leadership\",\"categories\":[\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"outsourcing\",\"innovation\",\"tech policy\",\"europe\",\"deep expertise\"],\"quote\":\"this excellent video talks about the problems with outsourcing everything\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PYylPRX6z4Q\",\"title\":\"Training AI Without Writing A Reward Function, with Reward Modelling\",\"channel\":\"Robert Miles AI Safety \",\"summary\":\"Robert Miles explains reward modelling, in which an AI learns what humans want from their preference comparisons instead of a hand-written reward function. He walks through the 2017 paper on deep reinforcement learning from human preferences.\",\"why_hn\":\"Seen as the clearest introduction to the idea behind RLHF, and also borrowed by commenters as a metaphor for human motivation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"reward modelling and rlhf\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"rlhf\",\"reinforcement learning\",\"ai safety\",\"reward function\",\"human preferences\"],\"quote\":\"I find this video by Rob Miles still the best presentation of the ingenious original 2017(!) paper\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PZbqAMEwtOE\",\"title\":\"Verbatim: What Is a Photocopier? | Op-Docs\",\"channel\":\"New York Times Opinion\",\"summary\":\"A New York Times Op-Docs short in which actors perform a real legal deposition transcript. A lawyer gets tangled in an absurd argument with a county employee about what counts as a photocopier, in a dispute over fees for public records.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters share it as a funny, memorable example of evasive depositions and of obstruction over public records requests.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"deposition reenactment\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"deposition\",\"public records\",\"dramatization\",\"legal absurdity\",\"nyt op-docs\"],\"quote\":\"The New York Times did an amazing reenactment from transcripts of the legal deposition\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"PdO3IP0Pro8\",\"title\":\"Thoth's Pill - an Animated History of Writing\",\"channel\":\"NativLang\",\"summary\":\"NativLang's 47-minute animated documentary traces the birth and evolution of writing, from early cave marks to modern scripts. It presents the history of the world's writing systems as a story.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fun, amazing overview of writing systems whenever the alphabet or cuneiform comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of writing\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"writing systems\",\"alphabet\",\"scripts\",\"linguistics\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"I also recommend Thoth's Pill, an amazing historical overview of writing systems\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Ps8jOj7diA0\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1 | Programming Paradigms (Stanford)\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of Stanford's CS107 Programming Paradigms, taught by Jerry Cain, giving an overview of the course. The course goes from C and assembly through C++, concurrency, Scheme and Python.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters rate the whole playlist as high-quality, clear teaching that builds foundations from memory and machine instructions up to different paradigms.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming paradigms\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Programming language design\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"stanford cs107\",\"jerry cain\",\"c programming\",\"assembly\",\"scheme\",\"python\"],\"quote\":\"The presentation is clear and it simplifies in just the right places to give you the foundations.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"QOEMv0S8AcA\",\"title\":\"Eben Moglen - Freedom in The Cloud\",\"channel\":\"Punkcast\",\"summary\":\"Eben Moglen speaks at NYU in 2010 about freedom in the cloud, arguing that centralized services and handing over personal data and server logs erode privacy and freedom. The talk lays out the motivation for FreedomBox, a personal server for your own data.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the foundational talk behind FreedomBox and the case against centralized web services.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital privacy and self-hosting\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"freedombox\",\"privacy\",\"cloud\",\"free software\",\"eben moglen\"],\"quote\":\"It's worth the watch/listen to get the idea in full.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"QRaACa1Mrd4\",\"title\":\"Why you DON'T want Perfect Pitch\",\"channel\":\"Adam Neely\",\"summary\":\"Adam Neely explains what perfect (absolute) pitch is, how it differs from relative and quasi-absolute pitch, and why he would not want it. He covers its drawbacks, including drift with age.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in threads on absolute pitch as a musician's useful summary of its downsides and age-related decline.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"perfect pitch\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"absolute pitch\",\"relative pitch\",\"music cognition\",\"ear training\",\"adam neely\"],\"quote\":\"This video by Adam Neely was helpful\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Qi1Yry33TQE\",\"title\":\"Ali Rahimi's talk at NIPS(NIPS 2017 Test-of-time award presentation)\",\"channel\":\"ObservantVids\",\"summary\":\"Ali Rahimi's NIPS 2017 Test-of-Time award talk, in which he argues that modern machine learning has become alchemy, working without being rigorously understood. He calls for more rigor and understanding in the field.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the sharpest,