le\":\"Walls, Floors, & Ceilings\",\"channel\":\"UncommentatedPannen\",\"summary\":\"Pannenkoek explains how Super Mario 64 handles wall, floor and ceiling collisions, including triangles, normal vectors, quarter steps and out of bounds. It is a detailed 37-minute technical walkthrough of the game's physics.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as an engrossing, in-depth look at SM64 mechanics that is surprisingly compelling to watch.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"super mario 64 collision mechanics\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"super mario 64\",\"collision detection\",\"hitboxes\",\"speedrunning\",\"pannenkoek\"],\"quote\":\"each longer than half an hour (and both of them extremely interesting)\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"V4V2bpZlqx8\",\"title\":\"Flaw in the Enigma Code - Numberphile\",\"channel\":\"Numberphile\",\"summary\":\"Numberphile's Dr James Grime explains the flaw in the Enigma machine, namely that a letter could never be encrypted to itself, and how that helped the Allies break the code. It touches on the Bombe and Alan Turing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a short, clear explanation of why Enigma was breakable and how search-space elimination works.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"enigma cryptanalysis\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"enigma\",\"cryptography\",\"world war ii\",\"alan turing\",\"bombe\"],\"quote\":\"Numberphile gives a nice, succinct explanation\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"VBwWbFpkltg\",\"title\":\"RailsConf 2019 - Opening Keynote by David Heinemeier Hansson\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson's opening keynote at RailsConf 2019 argues against the expectation of payment for open source work, drawing on his experience releasing Rails. It also includes lighter asides about the industry.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to reference in debates about whether open source maintainers are owed compensation, and commenters say it is worth watching even for non-Rails developers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"open source sustainability\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"dhh\",\"rails\",\"open source\",\"funding\",\"railsconf\"],\"quote\":\"Even if you're not a Rails developer it's worth watching.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"VDfX44fZoMc\",\"title\":\"J B  Rainsberger   Integrated Tests Are A Scam HD\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Cabanne\",\"summary\":\"J. B. Rainsberger argues that large integrated tests are a scam: they are slow, fail opaquely, and give a false sense of coverage. He advocates focused collaboration and contract tests at boundaries instead.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in testing-strategy debates as the classic case for fewer integration tests and more small, well-contracted unit tests.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software testing strategy\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"integration tests\",\"unit testing\",\"contract tests\",\"test design\",\"jb rainsberger\"],\"quote\":\"JBRainsberger has an nice talk on them [1].\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"VNvzZfsC13o\",\"title\":\"Are Electric Planes Possible?\",\"channel\":\"Real Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Real Engineering analyzes whether electric planes are feasible, focusing on battery energy density limits compared with jet fuel. It concludes electric flight works mainly for short ranges.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of why batteries limit electric aviation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electric aviation\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"electric planes\",\"batteries\",\"energy density\",\"aviation\",\"engineering\"],\"quote\":\"did a detailed analysis of the current operating limitations with electric planes\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"VO_1AdYRGW8\",\"title\":\"[4k, 60 fps] San Francisco, a Trip down Market Street, April 14, 1906\",\"channel\":\"Denis Shiryaev\",\"summary\":\"A neural-network upscaled 4K, 60 fps version of the 1906 film of a trip down Market Street in San Francisco, shot four days before the earthquake. It shows cable cars, horse carriages, early automobiles and pedestrians sharing the street.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads on cars, streets and urbanism as evidence of how mixed traffic worked before cars took over.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"historical street footage\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"san francisco\",\"1906\",\"film restoration\",\"mixed traffic\",\"urban history\"],\"quote\":\"You can see pedestrians, horse-drawn carriages, and early automobiles all \\\"flowing\\\" together.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"VPBH1eW28mo\",\"title\":\"The Only Safe Phone Is One Even the Police Can't Unlock\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"A five-minute CGP Grey explainer arguing that a phone is effectively an extension of your mind, and that any backdoor or master key into encrypted devices will inevitably be abused or leaked. It frames the encryption debate as locks that are either unbreakable or absent.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as a short, persuasive primer to send to non-technical people in debates over encryption backdoors and decryption legislation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"encryption backdoors\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Cryptography\"],\"tags\":[\"encryption\",\"backdoors\",\"privacy\",\"law enforcement\",\"smartphones\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey offers an easily digestible 5-minute primer\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"VeAdryYZ7ak\",\"title\":\"Practical Common Lisp\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Peter Seibel's 2006 Google TechTalk on Practical Common Lisp, framed by the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis and Paul Graham's Blub paradox. It shows Lisp features such as condition restarts and its generic-function object system.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN remembers it as the memorable explanation of restarts and multiple dispatch, referenced whenever those features come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"common lisp\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"common lisp\",\"multiple dispatch\",\"condition restarts\",\"peter seibel\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"I will forever think of this talk by Peter Siebel\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"WIyTZDHuarQ\",\"title\":\"Is This What Quantum Mechanics Looks Like?\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium shows silicone oil droplets bouncing on a vibrating bath, a physical analogue of pilot wave theory that reproduces quantum-like behavior such as double-slit patterns.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, intuitive introduction to the pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) interpretation of quantum mechanics.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pilot wave theory\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"pilot wave\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"double slit\",\"de broglie-bohm\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"an excellent pilot wave (de Broglie-Bohm) theory video by Veritasium[1]\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"WTYet-qf1jo\",\"title\":\"Windows 8: The Animated Evaluation\",\"channel\":\"blogphilofilms\",\"summary\":\"An animated, profane rant critiquing Windows 8, especially its touch-first interface on desktop PCs. The author draws on a background in tech journalism to lay out the UX problems.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked in Windows 8 threads as a funny but accurate summary of its usability problems.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"windows 8 ux\",\"categories\":[\"UX & UI design\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"windows 8\",\"ux critique\",\"animation\",\"rant\",\"metro interface\"],\"quote\":\"I think this video explains most of the problems with Windows 8\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"WlT3UhC7NwQ\",\"title\":\"Chase Adam at Startup School 2013\",\"channel\":\"Y Combinator\",\"summary\":\"Chase Adam, founder of the nonprofit Watsi, speaks at Startup School 2013 about building a nonprofit startup.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an excellent speech from Watsi's founder, especially on keeping a nonprofit aligned with the people it serves.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nonprofit startups\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\"],\"tags\":[\"watsi\",\"startup school\",\"nonprofit\",\"y combinator\",\"healthcare funding\"],\"quote\":\"Startup School presentation (which is excellent, BTW)\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"WxOp5mBY9IY\",\"title\":\"Macworld Boston 1997-The Microsoft Deal\",\"channel\":\"JoshuaG\",\"summary\":\"Footage from the Macworld Boston 1997 keynote where Steve Jobs announces Apple's deal with Microsoft, with Bill Gates appearing on the big screen. The almost bankrupt Apple took Microsoft's investment, and the crowd reacted badly, including booing over Internet Explorer as the default browser.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it as a legendary moment in tech history and as a precedent for strategic deals between rivals, such as in the Nokia and Microsoft story.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"apple microsoft 1997 deal\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"bill gates\",\"macworld 1997\",\"apple history\",\"keynote\"],\"quote\":\"the legendary video of Bill Gates appearing on the big screen at the Macworld Boston Keynote 1997\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"XKu_SEDAykw\",\"title\":\"How to: Work at Google — Example Coding/Engineering Interview\",\"channel\":\"Life at Google\",\"summary\":\"Two Google engineers perform a mock coding interview and then discuss best practices for candidates, including asking clarifying questions and communicating while solving.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a realistic, well-run example of a technical interview, both for candidates preparing and interviewers calibrating.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"coding interviews\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"interview\",\"whiteboard\",\"google\",\"hiring\",\"interview prep\"],\"quote\":\"two of the suggestions in this excellent video from Google showing an example of a great technical interview\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"XTeJ64KD5cg\",\"title\":\"Graham's Number - Numberphile\",\"channel\":\"Numberphile\",\"summary\":\"Numberphile's Tony Padilla and Matt Parker discuss Graham's number, once the largest number used in a mathematical proof, and how enormous it is.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the mind-melting intro to unimaginably large numbers.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"graham's number\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"graham's number\",\"large numbers\",\"ramsey theory\",\"numberphile\",\"up-arrow notation\"],\"quote\":\"It's a pretty interesting video for seeing how quickly the numbers get unimaginably massive.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Y0Oa4Lp5fLE\",\"title\":\"2. Behavioral Evolution\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"Stanford biologist Robert Sapolsky lectures on the evolution of behavior, including the Prisoner's Dilemma and tit-for-tat strategies in animals. It is part of his Human Behavioral Biology course.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in game-theory and cooperation threads, with the whole lecture series endorsed.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"behavioral evolution\",\"categories\":[\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Probability & game theory\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"robert sapolsky\",\"prisoner's dilemma\",\"tit for tat\",\"evolutionary biology\",\"cooperation\"],\"quote\":\"This entire lecture series on Human Behavioral Biology is worth watching from the beginning\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Y99Wj-NStok\",\"title\":\"[Epilepsy Warning] Choco Mountain: The History of Mario Kart 64's Most Infamous Track\",\"channel\":\"Summoning Salt\",\"summary\":\"Summoning Salt traces the speedrunning history of Mario Kart 64's Choco Mountain track and its famous shortcut, through the successive record holders and discoveries. It carries an epilepsy warning for flickering lights.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a gripping entry point into speedrunning and as a model of a well-made history video.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"speedrunning history\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"mario kart 64\",\"speedrunning\",\"choco mountain\",\"world records\",\"summoning salt\"],\"quote\":\"I didn't realize I cared about speed-running until I saw this YouTube video from Summoning Salt\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"YCjNT9qGjh4\",\"title\":\"Half-Life 2: 20th Anniversary Documentary\",\"channel\":\"Valve\",\"summary\":\"Valve's two-hour documentary for Half-Life 2's 20th anniversary, with team members discussing development, nearly running out of money, being hacked, the publisher lawsuit and the birth of Steam.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an excellent inside look at Valve's history, its technology and the games industry of the time.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"half-life 2 development\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"valve\",\"half-life 2\",\"steam\",\"game development\",\"game industry history\"],\"quote\":\"the excellent YouTube documentary Valve released for Half-Life 2's 20th Anniversary\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"YI3tsmFsrOg\",\"title\":\"The Deadliest Being on Planet Earth – The Bacteriophage\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"An animated Kurzgesagt explainer on bacteriophages, viruses that kill bacteria, covering how they infect and burst cells and their potential as therapy against antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it whenever phage therapy or antibiotic resistance comes up, as an accessible overview.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bacteriophages\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\