ers\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"_8bhtEgB8Mo\",\"title\":\"Black hole Firewalls - with Sean Carroll and Jennifer Ouellette\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"A Royal Institution event with Sean Carroll and Jennifer Ouellette on what happens if you fall into a black hole, covering the firewall paradox, the information problem and the holographic principle.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a high-level but clear explanation of the black hole information paradox and competing theories, by a physicist known for making quantum mechanics approachable.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"black hole information paradox\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"black holes\",\"firewalls\",\"holographic principle\",\"sean carroll\",\"quantum gravity\"],\"quote\":\"explains the paradox and several different competing theories at a high level.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"_DTpQ4Kk2wA\",\"title\":\"APL demonstration 1975\",\"channel\":\"Imperial College London\",\"summary\":\"A 1975 live demonstration of the APL language by Imperial College professor Bob Spence, showing a REPL on a modified typewriter terminal and APL's appeal to non-programmers.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a charming, from-zero introduction to APL and a window into early interactive computing.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"apl programming language\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"apl\",\"array languages\",\"computing history\",\"repl\",\"1975\"],\"quote\":\"I think this is a good youtube video for someone starting from zero\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"ab-r0capbzk\",\"title\":\"Did SARS-Cov-2 start in a Chinese lab?\",\"channel\":\"potholer54\",\"summary\":\"Science journalist potholer54 examines the claim that SARS-CoV-2 came from a Wuhan lab, tracing the sources behind it and the weaknesses in the evidence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a methodical debunking of early lab-leak claims.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid origins\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"sars-cov-2\",\"lab leak\",\"wuhan\",\"misinformation\",\"fact checking\"],\"quote\":\"Potholer54 did a rather methodical takedown\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"amJ-ev8Ial8\",\"title\":\"Salt: Are you getting Enough?   (More Sodium & Health)\",\"channel\":\"Joseph Everett - WIL\",\"summary\":\"Joseph Everett argues, drawing on the book The Salt Fix, that most people benefit from more rather than less salt, and reviews studies on sodium, potassium and blood pressure.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as a source of study references suggesting standard low-salt advice may be too restrictive.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dietary salt and health\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"sodium\",\"potassium\",\"blood pressure\",\"nutrition\",\"the salt fix\"],\"quote\":\"This is a nice video discussing some studies that show that high levels of salt is significantly better if it comes with high levels of potassium\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"ax7f3JZJHSw\",\"title\":\"What Games Are Like For Someone Who Doesn't Play Games\",\"channel\":\"Razbuten\",\"summary\":\"Razbuten has his wife, who has never played games, try a sampling of titles and shows where she struggles. It highlights unspoken game conventions that experienced players take for granted.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it to illustrate learned literacy in game and software UX, and how unfamiliar conventions confuse newcomers.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game literacy and ux\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"UX & UI design\",\"Gami