tasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explores compliant mechanisms, devices that move by flexing rather than using hinges and joints, with researchers at BYU. It covers advantages such as fewer parts and precision, including uses in nuclear weapon safety.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters like it as a high-quality, accessible look at an under-appreciated engineering idea and bring it up in snap-fit and hinge discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"compliant mechanisms\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"compliant mechanisms\",\"flexures\",\"mechanical engineering\",\"veritasium\",\"snap fit\"],\"quote\":\"Veritasium videos are pretty darn high quality.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"9OfBpQgLXUc\",\"title\":\"The Dutch Solution for Safer Sidewalks - Continuous Sidewalks\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"A short Not Just Bikes video on continuous sidewalks in the Netherlands, where the sidewalk runs unbroken across side streets so cars must visibly cross pedestrian space. It places them within Dutch sustainable safety and Vision Zero efforts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users point to it as a vivid example of how simple, intentional street design makes a big difference for pedestrian safety.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pedestrian street design\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"continuous sidewalks\",\"netherlands\",\"vision zero\",\"urban design\",\"road safety\"],\"quote\":\"talking about length of crossings on wide road, traffic signals, priorities, etc.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"9QveBbn7t_c\",\"title\":\"Monad - FunFunFunction #21\",\"channel\":\"Fun Fun Function\",\"summary\":\"An episode of FunFunFunction explaining monads in JavaScript as functors that also implement flatMap. It builds on the preceding functor episode and works through code examples.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended to people confused by monads as a friendly, practical explanation for JavaScript developers, with one commenter saying it finally made it click.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"monads in javascript\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"monads\",\"functional programming\",\"flatmap\",\"functors\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"I saw a video by MPJ/Funfunfunction [0] and it finally made sense.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"9R5OWh7luL4\",\"title\":\"Derren Brown - The System (Full)\",\"channel\":\"ScepticaTV\",\"summary\":\"Derren Brown's TV special in which he convinces a person he has a system for predicting horse race winners, demonstrating how survivorship bias and selective outcomes can fake predictive powers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the classic demonstration of survivorship and confirmation bias in betting, trading and prediction claims.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"survivorship bias\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"derren brown\",\"confirmation bias\",\"betting systems\",\"psychology\",\"trading\"],\"quote\":\"Here's an entertaining video from Derren Brown that shows how to use this technique to convince people you have psychic powers\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"9c8an2XZ3MU\",\"title\":\"Terence Mckenna - Culture is your operating system\",\"channel\":\"Dani Dögenigt\",\"summary\":\"A short edited excerpt of Terence McKenna arguing that culture acts as an operating system for the mind, set to imagery and music. It touches on psychedelics and how they change thinking.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as the origin of the 'culture/philosophy as operating system' metaphor and as a quick introduction to McKenna's ideas.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"culture as operating system\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"terence mckenna\",\"psychedelics\",\"culture\",\"consciousness\",\"philosophy\"],\"quote\":\"If you want to know where the metaphor of philosophy as an operating system comes from\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"9vpqilhW9uI\",\"title\":\"Why our IQ levels are higher than our grandparents' | James Flynn\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Moral philosopher James Flynn explains the Flynn effect, the steady rise of IQ scores across the 20th century. He argues the shift reflects a move toward abstract, hypothetical thinking as cognitively demanding professions grew.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of why generations think differently and why IQ scores have risen.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"flynn effect\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"iq\",\"james flynn\",\"abstract thinking\",\"cognitive history\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"This is an argument made by James Flynn in his amazing TED talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"9vz06QO3UkQ\",\"title\":\"The Internet's Own Boy: The Story of Aaron Swartz | full movie (2014)\",\"channel\":\"moviemaniacsDE\",\"summary\":\"The full documentary 'The Internet's Own Boy' about programmer and activist Aaron Swartz, with interviews with family, friends and internet figures. It covers his work on access to information and the legal case preceding his death.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the essential documentary for learning about Aaron Swartz and the case that shaped hacker culture.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"aaron swartz biography\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"aaron swartz\",\"open access\",\"cfaa\",\"internet activism\",\"hacker culture\"],\"quote\":\"here's a must-watch documentary, especially if you belong to the hacker culture\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AFVDZeg4RVY\",\"title\":\"[7] Jim Keller, Silicon Wizard 🧙‍♂️\",\"channel\":\"TechTechPotato\",\"summary\":\"Ian Cutress interviews chip architect Jim Keller about his time at AMD, Intel and elsewhere, management advice, design approach, Moore's Law and x86 versus Arm.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an interview dense with insight from Keller, worth hearing in full even for non-engineers.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"cpu architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"jim keller\",\"amd zen\",\"risc-v\",\"moore's law\",\"chip design\"],\"quote\":\"It’s actually important (if you’re not an engineer) to listen to the whole thing, because he drops knowledge all over the place.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AFnfTDbcPOA\",\"title\":\"CIA Officer in Charge of Taking Down the AQ Khan Network | James Lawler | Ep. 125\",\"channel\":\"The Team House\",\"summary\":\"A long Team House podcast interview with James Lawler, a former CIA operations officer who led counterproliferation work against the A.Q. Khan nuclear network. He discusses HUMINT, WMD and how such operations were run.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an in-depth look at the mechanics of intelligence work from a veteran who ran a nonproliferation operation.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cia counterproliferation\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"cia\",\"aq khan\",\"nuclear proliferation\",\"intelligence\",\"humint\"],\"quote\":\"if you want to hear someone who actively ran another incredibly successful nuclear non proliferation operation inside of CIA\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AItTqnTsVjA\",\"title\":\"The GUI should be better.  A lot better.\",\"channel\":\"Accursed Farms\",\"summary\":\"Accursed Farms' hour-plus critique of the modern desktop GUI. It argues that current interfaces are a regression from older designs such as Windows 7 and proposes principles for better ones.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thought-provoking critique that makes people notice how poor everyday GUIs are.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"desktop gui design\",\"categories\":[\"UX & UI design\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"gui\",\"user interface\",\"windows 7\",\"customization\",\"desktop\"],\"quote\":\"this video cursed me to pay far more attention on how shitty modern GUI is in my everyday interaction with the computer and phone\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"ALc8CBYOfkw\",\"title\":\"Do Complex Numbers Exist?\",\"channel\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder\",\"summary\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder asks whether complex numbers are just a convenient tool or necessary in physics. She discusses a recent paper arguing that quantum mechanics cannot be done with real numbers alone.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, accessible layperson explanation of the real-versus-complex quantum theory debate.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"complex numbers in quantum mechanics\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"complex numbers\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"physics\",\"sabine hossenfelder\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend video by Sabine Hossenfelder [1] on this topic.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AZX6awZq5Z0\",\"title\":\"Information, Evolution, and intelligent Design - With Daniel Dennett\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Daniel Dennett gives a Royal Institution talk toward a unified theory of information, tying together evolution, learning and engineering. He argues that design can arise bottom-up through small steps without a designer.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for its account of competence without comprehension, relevant to evolution and to debates about AI.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"information and evolution\",\"categories\":[\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"daniel dennett\",\"evolution\",\"intelligent design\",\"competence without comprehension\",\"philosophy of mind\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this Royal Institute talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AxCo2bE9Gtk\",\"title\":\"Republic, Lost (my favorite version)\",\"channel\":\"lessig\",\"summary\":\"Lawrence Lessig's 2011 Berkeley book talk based on Republic, Lost, arguing that money in politics creates institutional corruption in Congress. He calls it his favorite version of the talk.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it as a starting place for understanding how money corrupts American government.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"money in politics\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"lessig\",\"corruption\",\"congress\",\"campaign finance\",\"institutional corruption\"],\"quote\":\"Lawrence Lessig has some great talks about this topic, here's one of them\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"AxZWxxZ2JGE\",\"title\":\"Why our generals were more successful in World War II than in Korea, Vietnam or Iraq/Afghanistan\",\"channel\":\"UC Berkeley Events\",\"summary\":\"Thomas Ricks's Nimitz Memorial Lecture at UC Berkeley asks why US generals did better in World War II than in Korea, Vietnam or Iraq and Afghanistan. A central point is the WWII practice of quickly reassigning or relieving underperforming generals.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used in hiring and management debates as an analysis of fast evaluation, quick dismissal without stigma, and the cost of keeping underperformers.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"military leadership accountability\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"thomas ricks\",\"world war ii\",\"firing\",\"management\",\"hiring\",\"generals\"],\"quote\":\"Possibly the best analysis of this I have yet seen is the video\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"BDqvzFY72mg\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1: Introduction to Power and Politics in Today’s World\",\"channel\":\"YaleCourses\",\"summary\":\"The opening lecture of Ian Shapiro's Yale course Power and Politics in Today's World, which examines political dynamics and institutions over the past quarter century, including inequality, declining unions and new forms of authoritarianism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the lecture series as a foundational, wide-ranging grounding in modern global politics.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"modern political science\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"US politics & elections\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"ian shapiro\",\"yale\",\"politics\",\"inequality\",\"privatization\"],\"quote\":\"Yale's Power and Politcs was created by Ian Shapiro is an excellent zoom out into global politics and how how the modern world was made.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"B_nGEj8wIP0\",\"title\":\"Milton Friedman Speaks: Money and Inflation (B1230) - Full Video\",\"channel\":\"Free To Choose Network\",\"summary\":\"An 86-minute Milton Friedman talk arguing that inflation is a monetary phenomenon caused by money supply growing faster than goods, and that the cure is less government spending and money printing.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared during inflation discussions as an accessible, layman-friendly statement of the monetarist view.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"inflation and monetary policy\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"milton friedman\",\"inflation\",\"money supply\",\"monetarism\",\"economics\"],\"quote\":\"I think is under rated talk which explains complex stuff in layman terms.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"Bqx6li5dbEY\",\"title\":\"Ted Nelson in Herzog's \\\"Lo and Behold\\\"\",\"channel\":\"TheTedNelson\",\"summary\":\"A short excerpt from Werner Herzog's film Lo and Behold in which Ted Nelson lays out his ideas about hypertext and interconnection. Herzog concludes that Nelson is the only sane person in computing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN shares it as an accessible window into Ted Nelson's vision of Xanadu and linking, which commenters say gains meaning with historical context.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ted nelson and hypertext\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"ted nelson\",\"xanadu\",\"werner herzog\",\"hypertext\",\"lo and behold\"],\"quote\":\"I've grown to find this interview almost magical.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"BrK7X_XlGB8\",\"title\":\"A visual guide to Bayesian thinking\",\"channel\":\"Julia Galef\",\"summary\":\"Julia Galef uses pictures to explain Bayes' rule, then tells three personal stories showing how she applies it to improve her thinking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, accessible intro to Bayesian reasoning and priors.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bayesian reasoning\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"bayes rule\",\"probability\",\"rationality\",\"priors\",\"critical thinking\"],\"quote\":\"The most important mathematical video you will ever see in your life.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"C-kF25fWTO8\",\"title\":\"Clojure in a nutshell by James Trunk\",\"channel\":\"Func Prog Sweden\",\"summary\":\"James Trunk introduces Clojure syntax and thinking, covering its core data structures, immutability, the thread-last macro and live data analysis in the REPL.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a clear, enjoyable introduction to Clojure and a good example of REPL-driven development.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"clojure\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"repl-driven development\",\"functional programming\",\"immutability\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"gave one of the most clear and enjoyable technical introductions to Clojure I've seen. Recommended!\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"C8--cXwuuFQ\",\"title\":\"REcon 2013 - The Future of RE : Dynamic Binary Visualization (Christopher Domas)\",\"channel\":\"Sébastien Duquette\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Domas's REcon 2013 talk on dynamic binary visualization for reverse engineering. He shows how rendering binary data as interactive visual maps and trigraphs reveals structure that hex dumps hide, leading to his Cantor.dust tool.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters say it permanently changed how they think about inspecting binary data, and point to it as the origin of visual binary analysis tools.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"binary visualization for reverse engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"reverse engineering\",\"binary visualization\",\"cantor.dust\",\"security\",\"recon\"],\"quote\":\"after having seen Christopher Domas present \\\"The Future of RE: Dynamic Binary Visualization\\\" my mind was forever blown.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CJlrbMHLBd4\",\"title\":\"The Secret Life of the Vacuum Cleaner - Remastered\",\"channel\":\"tim hunkin\",\"summary\":\"Tim Hunkin's 1987 TV series episode on how vacuum cleaners and related household machines work, remastered and upscaled in 2021. It is part of the six-episode 'Secret Life of Machines' series with hands-on demonstrations of everyday technology.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, charming explanation of how machines work, including a demonstration of building an electric motor.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"how household machines work\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Engineering & making (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"tim hunkin\",\"secret life of machines\",\"vacuum cleaner\",\"electric motor\",\"engineering\"],\"quote\":\"I cannot recommend it strongly enough.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CK62I-4cuSY\",\"title\":\"Using Social Media To Cover For Lack Of Original Thought - Onion Talks - Ep. 6\",\"channel\":\"The Onion\",\"summary\":\"A four-minute Onion Talks parody in which a social media consultant pitches never putting any effort into social marketing. It lampoons TED-style talks and marketing consultants.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the Onion Talks parody most relevant to tech and startup culture.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social media satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"the onion\",\"parody\",\"ted talks\",\"social media marketing\",\"satire\"],\"quote\":\"I think the social media Onion talk ( ) is the most relevant to HN (and my favourite).\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CKIye4RZ-5k\",\"title\":\"World’s Best Speakers!\",\"channel\":\"Tech Ingredients\",\"summary\":\"Tech Ingredients shows how to build flat panel speakers from exciters and foam board, with construction guidelines, measured SPL comparisons and a complete system playing music.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a build guide for cheap DIY speakers that sound as good as expensive ones.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy speakers\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\"],\"tags\":[\"flat panel speakers\",\"exciters\",\"audio\",\"foam board\",\"diy\"],\"quote\":\"I built my own speakers for under \$200 and I honestly think they are some of the best I've ever heard for a personal setup.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CbIZU8cQWXc\",\"title\":\"Neil deGrasse Tyson - We Stopped Dreaming (Episode 1)\",\"channel\":\"Evan Schurr\",\"summary\":\"A short, music-driven video built from Neil deGrasse Tyson's remarks on why advancing the space frontier matters, contrasting NASA's small budget with war spending and stressing inspiration.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended to anyone asking whether space exploration is worth the cost.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"value of space exploration\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"neil degrasse tyson\",\"nasa budget\",\"space exploration\",\"inspiration\"],\"quote\":\"I always recommend anyone wondering about whether or not space exploration is worth it to view the following amazing video compilations.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CjVQJdIrDJ0\",\"title\":\"Thinking, Fast and Slow | Daniel Kahneman | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Daniel Kahneman's Talks at Google lecture on his book Thinking, Fast and Slow, covering System 1 and System 2 thinking and the psychology of judgment and decision making.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a one-hour substitute for the book's opening chapters for those short on time.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cognitive biases and decision making\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"daniel kahneman\",\"system 1 and 2\",\"behavioral economics\",\"psychology\",\"thinking fast and slow\"],\"quote\":\"If you're short on time, watch Kahneman's talk at Google, which basically summarizes the first chapter or so.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"CpCrkO1x-Qo\",\"title\":\"Tesla Model 3: Inside & Out - Autoline After Hours 417\",\"channel\":\"Autoline Network\",\"summary\":\"An Autoline After Hours episode with teardown expert Sandy Munro and a panel of auto journalists. It covers his Tesla Model 3 teardown, including the battery, drive modules, build quality and manufacturing automation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it in Tesla threads as an informed, hands-on engineering view of the Model 3 and its manufacturing.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"tesla model 3 teardown\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\"],\"tags\":[\"tesla\",\"sandy munro\",\"teardown\",\"automotive manufacturing\",\"battery packs\",\"build quality\"],\"quote\":\"If you're interested, this video is great\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"Cyya23MPoAI\",\"title\":\"Climbing SOLO with Alex Honnold   **INSANE EXPERIENCE**\",\"channel\":\"Magnus Midtbø\",\"summary\":\"Norwegian climber Magnus Midtbo spends a day climbing with Alex Honnold on exposed routes, shot as a vlog. The video shows the stark contrast between Midtbo's visible nerves and Honnold's calm.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for conveying viscerally just how exceptional Honnold's composure is compared to other elite climbers.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free solo climbing\",\"categories\":[\"Sports & athletics\"],\"tags\":[\"alex honnold\",\"climbing\",\"free solo\",\"magnus midtbo\",\"exposure\"],\"quote\":\"I need to share a video [0] which helped contextualize Alex Honnold for me\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"D5xh0ZIEUOE\",\"title\":\"James Mickens on JavaScript\",\"channel\":\"tablet reader\",\"summary\":\"James Mickens delivers his comedic rant about JavaScript and web programming, in his usual dry, hyperbolic style. It includes his account of learning the language from the O'Reilly Definitive Guide.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a hilarious talk by a beloved speaker, and also uses it to jab at JavaScript's quirks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript humor\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Satire & parody\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"james mickens\",\"javascript\",\"comedy\",\"web development\",\"tech talk\"],\"quote\":\"James Mickens is a national treasure. Go watch his talks on YouTube and read everything he writes.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"D7Sd8A6_fYU\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Dan Saks's CppCon 2016 talk on why C programmers resist C++ and how to talk to them about it. He argues that persuasion rarely works by arguing and examines the culture gap between the two communities.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in C versus C++ and embedded threads as the expert view on adoption and persuasion.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"c and c++ culture\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"c\",\"embedded\",\"cppcon\",\"language adoption\"],\"quote\":\"The mentioned cppcon presentation by Dan Saks is awesome. Highly recommend watching even if you are not interested in C or C++\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"DC8tO16xdrY\",\"title\":\"Oswald Acted Alone: JFK Assassination Solved (Part 1 of 2)\",\"channel\":\"Sean Munger\",\"summary\":\"The first part of a long two-part video series by historian Sean Munger arguing, with citations, that Lee Harvey Oswald assassinated President Kennedy and acted alone. It goes through the provable historical facts and addresses conspiracy theories.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the most exhaustive, well-sourced rebuttal of JFK conspiracy theories, often when new assassination claims surface.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"jfk assassination\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"jfk\",\"lee harvey oswald\",\"warren commission\",\"conspiracy theories\",\"american history\"],\"quote\":\"there's an exhaustive two part series by Sean Munger that sets out the many interlocking lines of evidence that Oswald acted alone.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"DJFKl_5JTnA\",\"title\":\"Donald B. Gillies Lecture: Dr. Michael Stonebraker\",\"channel\":\"Siebel School of Computing and Data Science\",\"summary\":\"Turing Award winner Michael Stonebraker gives the 2018 Donald B. Gillies Memorial Lecture at Illinois. He discusses the gap between academic research and industry problems, publishing incentives, and the influence of large industry users on research.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in many threads about academic incentives, paper counts and why academia struggles to produce working software.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"academic research incentives\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"stonebraker\",\"databases\",\"academia\",\"publishing incentives\",\"turing award\"],\"quote\":\"There's a lot more, I highly recommend the talk!\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"DkMwYxfSYNQ\",\"title\":\"The Why, What, and How of Pinning in Rust\",\"channel\":\"Jon Gjengset\",\"summary\":\"Jon Gjengset's long live-coding stream explains Rust's Pin and Unpin types: why they exist, how they work, and how they connect to futures and async/await. It also covers moving from pre-pinning code to the new model.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive resource for understanding Pin semantics and lists it among async Rust learning materials.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"rust pinning\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"rust\",\"pin\",\"unpin\",\"async await\",\"futures\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"Jon Gjengset's stream on pin/unpin is invaluable for understanding Pin semantics\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"E8Lhqri8tZk\",\"title\":\"1,500 Slot Machines Walk into a Bar: Adventures in Quantity Over Quality\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"In this 2019 GDC talk, Alex Schwartz and Ziba Scott explain how they flooded the app stores with over 1,500 auto-generated slot machine games to find the lowest bar for success. They tour the dark side of the mobile app market.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a hilarious account of mass-produced app store shovelware and cite it in threads on spam and low-quality content.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"app store shovelware\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Game design\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"mobile apps\",\"slot machines\",\"procedural generation\",\"gdc\",\"app store spam\"],\"quote\":\"They procedurally generated 1,500+ different iterations on top of the same Unity-based slots shovelware using noun/adjective pairs.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"EBCYvoC4muc\",\"title\":\"The Commodordion\",\"channel\":\"lftkryo\",\"summary\":\"Linus Akesson demonstrates the Commodordion, a fully functional 8-bit accordion built around Commodore 64 hardware with bellows made of floppy disks. He performs Scott Joplin's Maple Leaf Rag and tells the story of the build.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as the work of an unusually creative artist-engineer whose whole channel is worth exploring.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"c64 musical instruments\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"commodore 64\",\"accordion\",\"chiptune\",\"linus akesson\",\"hardware hacking\",\"ragtime\"],\"quote\":\"This dude's work is incredible. His entire YouTube channel is amazing.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"EZxP0i9ah8E\",\"title\":\"Paul Buchheit at Startup School 08\",\"channel\":\"startupschool\",\"summary\":\"Paul Buchheit, creator of Gmail and founder of FriendFeed, speaks at Startup School 08 about listening to users. He covers feedback, features, aiming for 100 happy users, and treating \\\"that's impossible\\\" as a statement of limited experience.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it for its memorable lines on advice, limited experience and early user focus, and rewatch it as a reminder not to be prescriptive.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"startup advice\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"paul buchheit\",\"user feedback\",\"startup school\",\"gmail\",\"friendfeed\"],\"quote\":\"I watch the video every once in a while to remind myself not to be similarly prescriptive\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.5},{\"vid\":\"E_xCssR8qQI\",\"title\":\"Prius Hybrid Drive Explained\",\"channel\":\"Niels Blaauw\",\"summary\":\"A Blender-animated explanation of the Toyota Hybrid Synergy Drive, showing how the